<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Project>

  <Import Project="Sdk.props" Sdk="Microsoft.DotNet.Arcade.Sdk" />

  <!-- Deliberately infrastructure-only.

       This file brings 16.11 onto Arcade's build/restore/CI plumbing and nothing else. It does not
       import main's policy: no analyzers, no LangVersion bump, no EnforceCodeStyleInBuild, no
       GenerateDocumentationFile, no net462/net8.0 TFM redefinitions. 16.11 keeps its own settings in
       scripts/build/TestPlatform.Settings.targets and its own package versions in
       scripts/build/TestPlatform.Dependencies.props. Old code, new infrastructure. -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- 2021-era framework packages carry known CVEs. A servicing line cannot be made CVE-free. -->
    <NuGetAudit>false</NuGetAudit>
    <!-- NETSDK1215 - targeting netstandard < 2.0 is no longer recommended. True, not fixable here.
         NU1504     - a package declared twice with the same version. Pre-existing, harmless, and an
                      error only under current NuGet.
         NU1603 is deliberately NOT suppressed. Silent version substitution is how a 16.11 package
         ends up shipping 17.x content; it caught NuGet trying to pull Fakes 17.12.0 into this line. -->
    <!-- NU1603 is deliberately NOT suppressed, and is promoted to an error explicitly here rather
         than relying on TreatWarningsAsErrors. The build runs with -warnAsError $false, which
         would otherwise demote it back to a warning, and a silent version substitution is exactly
         how Microsoft.CodeCoverage 17.14.2 resolved into a 15.9 build unnoticed. On this line it
         already caught NuGet trying to pull Fakes 17.12.0 into a 2021 servicing build. -->
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);NU1603</WarningsAsErrors>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);NETSDK1215;NU1504;NU1605;NU1701;NU1702</NoWarn>
    <!-- Servicing-age noise from building a 2021 line with a 2026 SDK. None of it is a product
         problem: NETSDK1138/1215 say the target frameworks are old, NETSDK1023 flags the MSTest
         reference this line pins on purpose, and CS1616/CS8105 are the signing key attribute
         duplicating CryptoKeyFile. MSB3276 is .NET Framework facade skew only (System.Runtime.*,
         System.Text.*, System.ValueTuple resolving from the SDK's net461 lib) - no vstest, MSTest
         or third-party product assembly is involved, so no version substitution is being hidden. -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);NETSDK1138;NETSDK1023;NETSDK1215;CS1616;CS8105</NoWarn>
    <MSBuildWarningsAsMessages>$(MSBuildWarningsAsMessages);MSB3276</MSBuildWarningsAsMessages>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- The single exception to the NU1603 promotion, scoped to one project.

       Microsoft.QualityTools.Testing.Fakes.TestRunnerHarness 16.11.230815 exists on no feed - it is
       the one external of 31 that cannot be resolved. Restore therefore substitutes 17.12.0 and
       NU1603 fails the build, which is the promotion working correctly on a substitution that cannot
       be fixed by restoring differently.

       Suppressing it here is safe for a specific, checkable reason rather than for convenience:
       packaging never consumes the restored package. scripts\build.ps1 reads the payload by
       versioned path -
         packages\Microsoft.QualityTools.Testing.Fakes.TestRunnerHarness\$(MicrosoftFakesVersion)\contentFiles
       - built from the pinned version in TestPlatform.Dependencies.props. The substituted 17.12.0
       lands in a different directory and is never read, so no 17.x content can reach the artifact.

       The compensating control is positive, not an assumption: VerifyFakesPayloadStaged below fails
       the build if the 16.11.230815 payload is absent. Without it this suppression would be exactly
       the "green bought by skipping" that costs coverage quietly - the check is what makes the
       difference between a known-safe substitution and an unexamined one. -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(MSBuildProjectName)' == 'external'">
    <WarningsAsErrors />
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);NU1603</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- Arcade defaults DebugType to 'embedded'. This line shipped separate .pdb files, so embedding
       is artifact-affecting rather than cosmetic: it removes every .pdb from the packages and
       changes the assemblies that carry the debug data.

       It first shows up as a script failure - scripts\build.ps1:409 cannot find
       src\testhost\bin\Release\net452\win7-x64\testhost.net452.pdb - which reads like another layout
       problem. It is not; the file was never produced.

       'portable' is what the spike tree used to reproduce the shipped artifact, verified by that
       tree emitting testhost.net452.exe, .exe.config and .pdb side by side. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <DebugType>portable</DebugType>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- Keep the historic output layout. Arcade would redirect every project to artifacts\bin\, which
       scripts/build.ps1 does not know about: its Publish-Package and the nuspecs read from
       src\<project>\bin\<Configuration>\<TFM>\. Preserving the layout means the Arcade build and the
       existing script-driven build produce the same tree, so adopting Arcade does not regress
       packaging. Adopting Arcade's own packing is a separate, later step. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <BaseOutputPath>$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)\bin\</BaseOutputPath>
    <BaseIntermediateOutputPath>$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)\obj\</BaseIntermediateOutputPath>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- Self-contained publish of netcoreapp1.0 fails NETSDK1061 on a current SDK: restore resolves
       Microsoft.NETCore.App 1.0.16 while the SDK's table would publish 1.0.5. Pin both to the
       version restore already chooses, so nothing about the built output changes. -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'netcoreapp1.0'">
    <RuntimeFrameworkVersion>1.0.16</RuntimeFrameworkVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- 16.11 ships 20 hand-written Properties\AssemblyInfo.cs files. Arcade generates the same
       attributes, which is CS0579 on every one of them. TestPlatform.Settings.targets already turns
       off some; these are the ones Arcade adds on top. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <GenerateAssemblyCopyrightAttribute>false</GenerateAssemblyCopyrightAttribute>
    <GenerateAssemblyCompanyAttribute>false</GenerateAssemblyCompanyAttribute>
    <GenerateAssemblyProductAttribute>false</GenerateAssemblyProductAttribute>
    <GenerateAssemblyConfigurationAttribute>false</GenerateAssemblyConfigurationAttribute>
    <GenerateNeutralResourcesLanguageAttribute>false</GenerateNeutralResourcesLanguageAttribute>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <!-- Sign config. Arcade defaults StrongNameKeyId to its Open key. 16.11 signs with the Microsoft
       key: all 1,858 managed assemblies in the shipped 16.11.0 packages carry public key token
       b03f5f7f11d50a3a, and the 67 source files that declare InternalsVisibleTo hard-code that same
       key. Letting Arcade substitute its own key is CS0281 on every friend access, and would also
       produce a strong name that does not match the shipped artifact. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <StrongNameKeyId>Microsoft</StrongNameKeyId>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- NET4X REFERENCE ASSEMBLIES, FOR THE TEST-ASSET BUILD ONLY.
       Gated on VsTestTestAssetsBuild, which ONLY the "Build test assets" pipeline step sets via
       /p:VsTestTestAssetsBuild=true. It is deliberately NOT unconditional:

       These product projects compile correctly through Arcade - the "Build" step is green. They fail
       only when reached as ProjectReferences from test\TestAssets\TestAssets.sln, which does not go
       through Arcade's entry point and so gets THIS root file instead of Arcade's net4x handling.
       Measured on build 3065423: MSB3644 x16 across exactly 8 product projects, each x2, with
       CS0518 x592 and CS0246 x240 downstream - no reference assemblies means no System.Object.

       APPLYING THIS UNCONDITIONALLY WOULD BE A PRODUCT CHANGE. It would alter which reference
       assemblies the SHIPPED product compiles against on a servicing branch whose mandate is to
       build shipped source unmodified, and the Arcade build does not need it.

       NO TargetFrameworkIdentifier / TargetFrameworkVersion CONDITION HERE, AND THAT IS THE POINT.
       An earlier version of this block conditioned each package on those two properties, copying the
       shape used in test\TestAssets\Directory.Build.props. Measured on build 3066460: it changed
       NOTHING - MSB3644 stayed at exactly 16, CS0518 at 592, CS0246 at 240, byte for byte the same
       counts as before the change. Those properties are set by the SDK's TargetFrameworkInference
       TARGETS, which import at the END of evaluation, while Directory.Build.props is imported by
       Microsoft.Common.props at the START. So the conditions were false and the ItemGroups never
       applied. A condition on a property that does not exist yet is not a narrower rule, it is a
       disabled one - and it fails silently, with no warning and no diff.

       The meta-package with IncludeAssets="build" is used instead, which needs no TFM condition.
       test\TestAssets\Directory.Build.props records why IncludeAssets matters: with it the restore
       graph carries the meta-package AND its per-framework children; without it, the meta-package
       only, and the children's build assets are what supply the reference assemblies. -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(VsTestTestAssetsBuild)' == 'true'">
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NETFramework.ReferenceAssemblies" Version="1.0.3" PrivateAssets="All" IncludeAssets="build" IsImplicitlyDefined="true" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
